# S4d (wt-rv2fix), low: the copy of the old value is decided by comparing paths (storageWithinPlace: same root variable,
# same members), so a borrow of the place through ANOTHER reference to the same instance is not seen. Here set(x, x):
# "p.a = E.Neg(q.a)" borrows q.a, which is p.a, and the value written over p.a in place holds p.a itself. Statically
# undecidable in general (it is aliasing); S4d's "no assignment makes a value hold its own storage" over-promises -
# its last sentence ("with r a reference to x, x = E.Neg(r) makes the cycle it says") should cover a borrow written
# through a reference too. Not a memory hazard: a cycle, the same in every mode.
# expected (as specified): -5
# actual:   -b and -d: segmentation fault (eval recurses); -i: "it recurses deeper than -i allows"
type E enum {
    Lit(v I64)
    Neg(e E&)
}
type PE struct(a E) {
    a
}
fn eval(e E&) I64 {
    return match e {
        case E.Lit(v) => v
        case E.Neg(x) => 0 - eval(x)
    }
}
fn set(p mut PE&, q PE&) {
    p.a = E.Neg(q.a)
}
fn main() ? {
    x := PE(E.Lit(5))
    set(x, x)
    println($eval(x.a))
}
